-- allium: 3
-- feed-scripts.allium
--
-- Scope: The reference feed-script templates dispatch ships under `scripts/`.
--        What each one queries, which config files it reads, which Signals and
--        labels it emits, and why each decision is the script's rather than the
--        runtime's.
-- Excludes: The feed runtime itself — the FeedItem wire format, the Signal
--           vocabulary, routing, upsert semantics, polling cadence, the
--           verify-feed validator and setup seeding (all in feeds.allium); the
--           domain model (see core.allium).
--
-- These templates are USER-OWNED EXECUTABLES, not part of the feed runtime.
-- feeds.allium's own scope line excludes them, and correctly: the generic
-- runtime never embeds upstream-specific knowledge. But the templates were
-- specified inside feeds.allium anyway, under a header that said they were
-- not there. This file is where that material belongs — normative for anyone
-- editing `scripts/`, and out of the runtime spec's way.
--
-- Extracted from feeds.allium (task #4738).

-- The dispatch repo ships reference templates under `scripts/`:
--
--   - scripts/fetch-dependabot.sh — open dependency-update PRs via
--                                   `gh pr list --author`, one pass per bot
--                                   login in bots.conf's BOT_AUTHORS. It reads
--                                   the SAME bots.conf that fetch-reviews.sh's
--                                   bot-author pass reads, rather than naming
--                                   one bot itself: a bot's login is
--                                   deployment-specific, and a template that
--                                   hardcodes one org's Renovate app silently
--                                   misses that deployment's Dependabot PRs.
--                                   An absent or empty BOT_AUTHORS falls back
--                                   to app/kognic-renovate, so an existing
--                                   copy keeps emitting what it emitted before
--   - scripts/fetch-cve.sh        — open Dependabot vulnerability alerts
--                                   (CVEs) via the `gh api` Dependabot
--                                   endpoint, for the managed CVE feed (see
--                                   "Managed review & CVE feeds" in
--                                   docs/reference.md)
--   - scripts/fetch-security.sh   — the same open Dependabot vulnerability
--                                   alerts, predating the managed CVE feed;
--                                   kept for a user wiring a hand-rolled
--                                   generic epic instead of cve_feed_command
--   - scripts/fetch-reviews.sh    — every open PR the user is involved with,
--                                   as a SINGLE deduped FeedItem array (see
--                                   below)
--   - scripts/fetch-log-warnings.sh — distinct WARN/ERROR records in a
--                                   tracing-formatted log file, one FeedItem
--                                   per distinct record rather than per line.
--                                   The only shipped template for an
--                                   APPEND-ONLY epic (see feeds.allium:
--                                   AppendOnlyFeed); see "events, not
--                                   state" below
--
-- The first four templates ship with empty REPOS placeholders, and
-- fetch-log-warnings.sh with an empty LOG_FILE placeholder; all five are
-- inert until the user edits them. They are reference implementations, not
-- part of the feed runtime.
--
-- == scripts/fetch-log-warnings.sh: events, not state ==
--
-- The other four templates mirror an upstream that has an open/closed state.
-- This one does not: a log record is an event, so its epic sets
-- core/Epic.feed_append_only (AppendOnlyFeed) and the script never has to
-- represent "still a problem".
--
-- The whole design question for such a script is what makes two log lines
-- THE SAME PROBLEM, because that is what external_id has to capture. A raw
-- line is useless — every line differs by timestamp, and a real log holds
-- six-figure counts of a handful of distinct problems. The template
-- fingerprints each record as its emitting MODULE TARGET plus the STATIC HEAD
-- of its message (up to the first ":" or " key="), which is a proxy for the
-- call site that survives refactoring, unlike a file:line. Everything after
-- that head is the interpolated detail that varies per occurrence.
--
-- The item's `url` is the repo root ("https://github.com/<org>/<repo>"), a
-- form extract_github_repo already accepts, so UpsertFeedTasks resolves a
-- repo_path and the task is dispatchable. A log record carries no upstream
-- URL of its own, and this is the whole reason the field is set.
--
-- The description carries the occurrence count, first and last seen
-- timestamps and a sample of raw lines. Those are re-applied on every poll
-- (UpsertFeedTasks refreshes description on conflict), so an open card keeps
-- a current count while the user works on it.
--
-- Every item sets wrap_up_mode: "rebase". A triage card has exactly three
-- resolutions (a real bug fixed, a wrong log line demoted, a transient record
-- deleted) and the first two are both a small change landing on the repo the
-- log came from — there is no upstream to open a PR against, and no third
-- party to review a one-line level change. Leaving the field absent made every
-- card ask the same question at wrap-up time and get the same answer. The
-- preference is applied on INSERT only (see FeedItem.wrap_up_mode in
-- feeds.allium), so a user who changes a single card's path keeps that change
-- across later polls.
--
-- WHY THIS IS A TEMPLATE AND NOT A RUNTIME FEATURE. Both halves of it are
-- policy: which log file, and what counts as one problem. The tracing format
-- the template parses is dispatch's own; a user pointing this at a different
-- repo's logs edits the fingerprint recipe. Nothing about log scanning
-- reaches the runtime — the runtime learned only that a feed may be
-- append-only, which is upstream-agnostic.
--
-- == scripts/fetch-cve.sh: an alert names the open PR that fixes it ==
--
-- Most alerts already have a fix waiting: Dependabot opens a security update
-- PR for its own alert, and Renovate opens a bump for the same package. A
-- CVE task that ignores that PR has its agent write a second, competing fix.
-- So fetch-cve.sh looks for an open PR in the alert's own repo and, when it
-- finds one, the task's job becomes reviewing and merging that PR (see
-- RenderCveRunbook in dispatch-prompt.allium) instead of authoring a fix.
--
-- HOW A PR IS FOUND, in precedence order. The first source that yields an
-- open PR wins; later sources are not consulted for that alert.
--
--   1. THE ALERT'S OWN LINK. GitHub's GraphQL alert node carries
--      `dependabotUpdate.pullRequest`: the security update PR Dependabot
--      opened for exactly this alert. It is authoritative, so it is used
--      whenever that PR is OPEN. A merged or closed one links nothing.
--   2. A BOT PR THAT NAMES THE ADVISORY. An open, non-draft, bot-authored PR
--      whose title or body contains the advisory's GHSA id or CVE id.
--      Renovate's vulnerability PRs cite the advisory this way, and Renovate
--      PRs never appear in source 1.
--   3. A BOT PR THAT NAMES THE PACKAGE. An open, non-draft, bot-authored PR
--      whose title holds the vulnerable package's name as a whole token
--      (case-insensitive; the title split on whitespace, commas, brackets,
--      parentheses and backticks). Whole-token, so "rand" never matches a
--      bump of "random". Within sources 2 and 3 the newest PR (highest
--      number) wins.
--
-- "Bot-authored" is gh's own `author.is_bot`, not bots.conf: the question is
-- "did a dependency bot open this", and every such bot answers it, where
-- bots.conf lists only the bots whose PRs earn the dependency REVIEW runbook.
-- Sources 2 and 3 skip human PRs on purpose: a person's PR that mentions a
-- package is not a fix to merge without reading it.
--
-- Sources 2 and 3 are a heuristic. A package-name match does not prove the
-- PR lands on a fixed version, and the runbook does not assume it does: the
-- agent still checks the PR's resulting version against the advisory's
-- fixed set before approving anything.
--
-- WHAT A LINKED ITEM CARRIES. Its description gains a final paragraph
-- "Fix PR: <url> (<title>)", its labels gain "fix-pr", and its wrap_up_mode
-- is "done" instead of "pr" — merging another author's PR leaves the agent
-- no commits of its own to open a PR for. The url stays the ALERT (url_type
-- security_alert): the runbook reads the advisory from it, and the feed's
-- Reconcile sync already removes the task once the merged PR closes the
-- alert, so nothing needs to track the PR's own state.
--
-- Description and labels are re-applied on every poll, so a PR that appears
-- after the card was created links it on the next poll, and one that closes
-- unmerged unlinks it. wrap_up_mode is applied on INSERT only (see
-- FeedItem.wrap_up_mode in feeds.allium), so a card linked later keeps "pr";
-- the runbook tells the agent which wrap-up path to take either way.
--
-- FAILURE DEGRADES TO UNLINKED. Each repo costs one paginated GraphQL query
-- (source 1) and one `gh pr list --state open --limit 200` (sources 2 and 3),
-- tried with each authenticated token like the alerts call. If either fails,
-- the script warns on stderr and emits that repo's alerts without the links
-- the failed call would have supplied. It never drops an alert for it: an
-- unlinked alert is still correct work, while a missing one deletes its task
-- under Reconcile. A PR list that hits its limit also warns, since a fix PR
-- past the cut goes unlinked.
--
-- fetch-security.sh does NOT link PRs. Its tasks sit on an ordinary epic,
-- which never renders the CVE runbook (CveRemediationSkipsTheDesignStep), so
-- a "Fix PR" line there would come with no instruction to act on it.
--
-- == scripts/fetch-reviews.sh: single emission with routing signals ==
--
-- fetch-reviews.sh emits ONE deduped FeedItem array covering every open PR
-- the user is involved with — the union of these `gh search prs --state=open`
-- queries, each matched PR tagged with the Signal it matched:
--
--   - review-requested:@me        -> signal team_request   (direct + team)
--   - user-review-requested:@me   -> signal direct_request (direct only)
--   - reviewed-by:@me             -> signal reviewed
--   - commenter:@me -author:@me   -> signal commented (excludes the user's
--                                    own PRs)
--
-- These four queries are scoped by REPO (the same `repos.conf` REPOS list
-- fetch-cve.sh reads) and feed all three sub-epics (My/Team/Bots) exactly as
-- before. THREE of the four qualifiers are ALSO run a second time, scoped by
-- GitHub ORG instead, via a separate `org.conf` (ORGS array) read only by
-- this second pass — repo-scope and org-scope are deliberately kept in
-- different config files so widening "my reviews" to whole orgs doesn't
-- accidentally widen the Team/Bots queries too. Every org-scoped match
-- routes to my_reviews (see RouteSignals rule 3) rather than leaking into
-- team_reviews, whichever qualifier matched:
--
--   - reviewed-by:@me | commenter:@me -author:@me
--                                 (per org in org.conf) -> signal org_review
--   - user-review-requested:@me   (per org in org.conf) -> signal
--     direct_request, NOT org_review. See "The org-scoped personal-request
--     qualifier keeps its own signal" below for why this one is the
--     exception.
--
-- review-requested:@me is DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED from the org-scoped pass: it
-- matches PRs requested from a team the user belongs to, not just PRs
-- requested from the user personally (unlike the other three qualifiers,
-- which are always personal). Org-wide, that team-inclusive match would
-- sweep in far more team-request noise than the repo-scoped pass ever did,
-- so the org-scoped pass only fires for qualifiers that are unambiguously
-- about the user themselves.
--
-- A FIFTH pass covers BOT-AUTHORED PRs the user has no review involvement
-- with at all. The four review qualifiers above are all about the user, so a
-- Renovate or Dependabot PR reached the bots sub-epic only when the user
-- happened to be a requested reviewer on it — which for dependency-update PRs
-- is the exception, not the rule. This pass queries by AUTHOR instead:
--
--   - author:<bot>  (per bot author in bots.conf's BOT_AUTHORS and
--     RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS, per repo in repos.conf) -> signal author_bot
--
-- It emits author_bot as its query signal rather than introducing a new
-- Signal variant: route({author_bot}) already yields bots (RouteSignals rule
-- 2), so the existing routing contract carries this pass unchanged. A bot PR
-- the user HAS reviewed or commented on still merges signals into one item
-- and still routes to my_reviews — engagement wins over the bot rule
-- (RouteSignals rule 1), and that precedence is deliberately not disturbed
-- here.
--
-- Bot logins live in a THIRD config file, `bots.conf` (the BOT_AUTHORS and
-- RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS arrays), read only by this pass and by
-- fetch-dependabot.sh, which lists PRs by BOT_AUTHORS and reads
-- RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS only to refuse an entry on both lists (see "A bot on
-- both lists is fatal" below). They are a separate scope from repos.conf/org.conf
-- for the same reason those two are separate from each other, and because a
-- bot's login is deployment-specific (a self-hosted Renovate app is named
-- after its installing org, e.g. `app/kognic-renovate`, not `app/renovate`).
-- The pass queries the two lists together; only when BOTH are absent or empty
-- is it skipped entirely.
--
-- Scope is REPO-ONLY: this pass is never run org-scoped. Org-wide bot
-- authorship would sweep in every dependency-update PR in the org, orders of
-- magnitude more than the review passes emit.
--
-- The pass is capped PER REPO, not per query: one `gh pr list --repo <repo>
-- --author <bot> --state open --limit 20 --search sort:created-desc` call per
-- (repo x bot author, either list), keeping the newest bumps. A single
-- multi-repo call with one `--limit` would cap the TOTAL, letting one busy
-- repo starve every other repo's bot PRs out of the emission.
--
-- The pass uses `gh pr list`, NOT `gh search prs`, to stay off GitHub's REST
-- search pool (30 requests/min, plus secondary burst limits). The per-(repo x
-- author) split costs len(REPOS) * len(authors) calls a poll — 20 for a
-- 10-repo, 2-bot deployment — and as `gh search prs` calls those, fired back
-- to back after the seven review searches, tripped GitHub's secondary rate
-- limit every busy cycle (task 77856). `gh pr list --author` runs its search
-- through GraphQL, a separate 5000-point/hour budget, so only the seven review
-- searches draw on the search pool and the no-starvation split costs nothing
-- there.
--
-- The two commands report a bot author in different forms: `gh search prs`
-- gives `<slug>[bot]`, `gh pr list` gives `app/<slug>`, flagged as a bot.
-- The pass rewrites the latter to `<slug>[bot]` before the shared item mapper
-- sees it, so the author_bot signal, the tag and the `@<login>` label are
-- identical whichever pass found the PR. `gh pr list` also has no
-- `repository` field; the pass fills it from the repo it queried.
--
-- `gh pr list --author` returns `[]` and exits 0 for a repo that does not
-- exist or that the token cannot see, where `gh search prs` fails. So the pass
-- probes each repo first (`gh api repos/<repo>`, the core pool) and, when the
-- probe fails, says so on stderr and skips that repo. An unreachable repo must
-- stay a LOUD failure: the stderr line is what makes the cycle sync
-- additively, so an SSO lapse keeps that repo's bot cards rather than reading
-- as "no open bot PRs" and removing them.
--
-- Plus per-PR author signals, derived from the PR author login:
--
--   - author_bot when the login ends in `[bot]` (Renovate/Dependabot)
--   - author_me  when the login is the gh user
--
-- Dedup MERGES (unions) the per-PR signal arrays when a PR matches several
-- queries: a PR appears EXACTLY ONCE carrying ALL the signals it matched.
-- The jq idiom is `group_by(.url) | map(.[0] + {signals: (map(.signals[]) |
-- unique)})`, NOT `unique_by`, which would keep one representative and discard
-- the other queries' signals.
--
-- Bot-authored PRs are INCLUDED (Renovate/Dependabot are no longer excluded),
-- both when a review query matches them and, via the bot-author pass above,
-- when nothing else does. A PR authored by one of RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS' bots
-- carries `tag "release-please"` (see "Release bots get their own tag"
-- below). Otherwise, WHERE BOT_AUTHORS IS CONFIGURED, a PR authored by one of
-- its bots carries `tag "dependabot"` and every other PR, bot-authored or not,
-- carries `tag "pr-review"`; where it is empty — the shipped state — the
-- dependabot tag falls back to the "[bot]" login suffix. See "Which bot earns
-- the dependency runbook" below.
-- Draft PRs are INCLUDED (not filtered by routing), and carry an extra
-- "draft" entry in `labels` so the TUI card surfaces draft status as a
-- badge (see cards.rs render_card_indicator, which renders every `labels`
-- entry as a `[label]` badge — no dedicated draft-badge code path needed).
--
-- == CI status label ==
--
-- Every emitted PR also carries its CI status as a `labels` entry, so the
-- card says whether the PR is mergeable without the user opening it. The
-- vocabulary is exactly three strings:
--
--   - "ci:pass"    the PR head commit's check rollup succeeded
--   - "ci:fail"    the rollup failed or errored
--   - "ci:pending" the rollup is still running, or expected but not started
--
-- A PR with NO checks at all gets NO ci label — absence of the badge means
-- "nothing ran", which is distinct from all three states above and must not
-- be collapsed into "pass". These three strings are a wire vocabulary shared
-- with the card renderer, which colours them (see "Card label badges" in
-- board-visuals.allium); a fourth value would render as a plain badge, not fail.
--
-- Every uncertain outcome collapses to the SAME absent label: no checks, an
-- upstream status value this vocabulary does not cover, and a failed status
-- fetch (below) all leave the PR unlabelled. "We do not know" therefore looks
-- exactly like "nothing ran". That conflation is accepted deliberately: the
-- alternative is a fourth badge meaning "unknown", which costs a card row of
-- attention to say nothing, and the one outcome that must never be guessed is
-- a green badge on a red PR.
--
-- The label applies to EVERY emitted PR, in all three role sub-epics. A bot
-- PR's CI status is if anything the most useful of the three: a green
-- Renovate bump is the one that can be merged without reading it.
--
-- The status is a SEPARATE FETCH. `gh search prs --json` exposes no check
-- field at all, so the review and bot-author passes above cannot carry it.
-- After dedup, the script resolves the status for the whole deduped set and
-- merges the label in. That resolution is BATCHED — many PRs per request, not
-- one request per PR: the per-PR cost would scale with the bot backlog, which
-- the bot-author pass deliberately allows to reach 20 per (repo x bot). The
-- batch size is a script constant, so an ordinary emission costs one request
-- and only a very large one costs a second; the number of requests is a cost
-- property, not a behavioural one.
--
-- A FAILED status fetch degrades to NO ci label on the affected PRs, never to
-- a wrong one and never to a failed emission. CI status is decoration on an
-- item whose identity and routing are already fully determined; losing it
-- must not cost the user the review card itself. The failure goes to stderr,
-- which FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess surfaces without failing the cycle.
--
-- == The approved signal ==
--
-- Every emitted PR that the user has approved carries an `approved` signal.
-- feeds.allium's ExcludeFromReviews contract reads it to decide whether the PR
-- is still review work; this script only states the fact.
--
--   - approved   the user's LATEST OPINIONATED review on the PR is an
--                approval. An opinionated review approves or requests changes;
--                a plain comment is neither and does not displace an earlier
--                approval. A dismissed approval does not count.
--
-- It is NOT derivable from the review queries. `reviewed-by:@me` matches a PR
-- the user approved and one the user requested changes on identically, and no
-- search qualifier distinguishes them. So it comes from the PR's own review
-- state, resolved AFTER dedup for the whole deduped set — the same place and
-- the same batched request the CI status uses, since that already visits every
-- emitted PR exactly once. Sharing the request is a cost decision, not a
-- behavioural one.
--
-- WHAT THIS RESOLUTION DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FETCH: the PR's pending-reviewer
-- list. "A review is currently requested from me" is already a fact the
-- searches produce — direct_request and team_request — so reading it here
-- would be a second derivation of one fact. It would also be an expensive one:
-- the rate-limit cost of a GraphQL request scales with the product of the page
-- sizes down the connection tree, so a bounded reviewer list under a batch of
-- PRs multiplies the cost of EVERY batch, and the bound brings a truncation
-- failure mode of its own. See feeds.allium: "WHY THE RESCUE IS THE REQUEST
-- SIGNALS AND NOT A FETCHED REVIEWER LIST".
--
-- A FAILED resolution degrades to NO approved signal on the affected PRs,
-- never to a wrong one and never to a failed emission — the same rule the CI
-- label follows. The failure direction matters and is deliberate: a missing
-- approved signal shows a settled PR again, which errs toward VISIBLE, so no
-- degradation can hide outstanding review work. The failure goes to stderr,
-- which FeedCommandStderrOnSuccess surfaces without failing the cycle.
--
-- == The login lookup is load-bearing, and says so when it fails ==
--
-- The user's login is needed for the approved signal and for author_me alike.
-- That lookup soft-fails to empty rather than failing the feed. When it has
-- failed, the review-state resolution is SKIPPED rather than guessed: no
-- approved signal on any item that cycle, so nothing is excluded on approval
-- grounds until the next successful poll.
--
-- That failure WRITES TO STDERR, like every other soft failure in these
-- templates. It used to be the one silent one, which was tolerable while it
-- cost only author_me. It now also suppresses the approved signal, so a user
-- whose login lookup is failing sees both their own PRs and their approved
-- PRs come back with nothing anywhere saying why.
--
-- Both "is this login me?" tests — author_me, and the login the review-state
-- query is scoped to — normalise case. GitHub logins are case-preserving but
-- unique case-insensitively, so an exact match silently misses whenever the
-- two spellings differ.
--
-- == The org-scoped personal-request qualifier keeps its own signal ==
--
-- The org-scoped pass runs three qualifiers. Two of them — reviewed-by:@me and
-- commenter:@me -author:@me — collapse into the single org_review signal.
-- user-review-requested:@me does NOT: it emits direct_request, the same signal
-- its repo-scoped twin emits.
--
-- WHY IT IS THE EXCEPTION. It states a fact the other two do not: a review is
-- pending from the user personally, right now. That fact is what rescues an
-- already-approved PR from being hidden (feeds.allium's ExcludeFromReviews).
-- Collapsed into org_review, an org-wide re-request after the user's approval
-- would be indistinguishable from "I once commented on this", and the card
-- would never come back — a silent hole in the exclusion, in exactly the repos
-- the org scope exists to cover.
--
-- NOTHING ELSE CHANGES. Routing is identical: direct_request and org_review
-- both yield my_reviews (RouteSignals rule 3) and both lose to the bot rule.
-- The org pass's guarantee — every match lands in My Reviews, never Team or
-- Bots — is unaffected.
--
-- review-requested:@me is still excluded from the org-scoped pass entirely:
-- unlike the other three it is team-inclusive, and org-wide that would sweep
-- in team-request noise.
--
-- The script takes NO scope argument. It emits the full involved-PR set once;
-- the dispatch role router (feed_role = reviews_parent) splits that single
-- emission into the My / Team / Bots sub-epics using the per-item signals (see
-- feeds.allium's RouteSignals contract). This folds the old fetch-dependabot.sh
-- PR-fetching into the reviews emission — fetch-dependabot.sh is retained here
-- for reference, but its bot PRs are now surfaced through fetch-reviews.sh's
-- author_bot signal, and the bot-author pass above makes that fold complete:
-- reaching the bots sub-epic no longer depends on review involvement.
--
-- So there are TWO shipped producers of a task tagged dependabot, in two
-- different scripts, and both reach the same runbook:
--
--   - fetch-dependabot.sh, whose every emitted item carries tag dependabot;
--   - THIS script's to_feed_items, which tags an item dependabot when the PR's
--     author is one of bots.conf's BOT_AUTHORS (or, where that list is empty,
--     when the author login ends in "[bot]"), release_please when the author
--     is one of RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS, and pr_review otherwise.
--
-- The second is what makes the DEPENDABOT title form live rather than
-- fallback-only: a bots.conf listing app/dependabot puts genuine
-- "Bump <pkg> from <X> to <Y>" titles on the board through either script.
-- Anything reading the dependabot queue must expect both bots' title
-- conventions; see AReviewRunbookCarriesOnlyTheBranchThatApplies in
-- dispatch-prompt.allium.
--
-- == Which bot earns the dependency runbook ==
--
-- to_feed_items matches the AUTHOR against BOT_AUTHORS. It used to ask only
-- whether the login ended in "[bot]", which conflated "written by a bot" with
-- "is a dependency update" — and those are not the same set. A release bot
-- (release-please and its kin, authoring "chore(master): release 8.21.10")
-- ends in "[bot]" like any other, so its PRs were handed the dependency
-- review runbook, which classifies a bump they do not contain and can only
-- ever route them to the user as unclassified. The tag drives the runbook, so
-- it has to mean what the runbook assumes.
--
-- BOT_AUTHORS is the list that already means "dependency bot" — it is what
-- fetch-dependabot.sh lists PRs by — so no second config knob is introduced
-- to express the same fact. Its entries are gh's app form ("app/<slug>") and
-- the PR author login is "<slug>[bot]", so the match normalises between the
-- two rather than comparing them literally.
--
-- A bot NOT on the list keeps its PR: it is tagged pr_review and reaches the
-- board through whichever review query matched it. Nothing is dropped. A
-- release bot listed in RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS instead gets its own tag and
-- runbook (see "Release bots get their own tag" below); an unlisted one still
-- gets the PR-review runbook, which wants a human.
--
-- ONLY THE TAG NARROWS. The author_bot SIGNAL still comes from the "[bot]"
-- login suffix, because the two answer different questions: the signal says
-- who wrote the PR, which is what routes it to the Bots sub-epic, and a
-- release bot is a bot. The tag says which runbook fits it, which is a claim
-- about the PR's content. So a release bot's PR routes to Bots as it always
-- did and gets the PR-review runbook there — or, when RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS
-- lists it, the release-PR runbook.
--
-- AN EMPTY OR ABSENT BOT_AUTHORS FALLS BACK to the "[bot]" suffix test: the
-- narrowing is only sound once the deployment has SAID which of its bots are
-- dependency bots, and until it has, the suffix is the best proxy available
-- and is what this script always used.
--
-- BE PLAIN ABOUT WHAT THAT COSTS. An empty list is the SHIPPED state, so an
-- unconfigured deployment keeps the exact behaviour this rule exists to fix —
-- its release bot's PRs still reach the dependency runbook. This narrowing
-- fixes one emitter in one configuration; fetch-dependabot.sh tags every item
-- it emits unconditionally and is unaffected either way. The boundary is
-- deliberate, not complete.
--
-- Two shapes were considered and deferred rather than rejected. A separate
-- DEP_BOT_AUTHORS defaulting to BOT_AUTHORS would let a deployment sweep a
-- release bot's PRs onto the board WITHOUT arming the dependency runbook for
-- them, which the single list cannot express — hence bots.conf's "do not list
-- a release bot here". RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS (task 53281) now gives that
-- capability back from the other side: a release bot is listed THERE, which
-- sweeps its PRs onto the board under their own tag and leaves BOT_AUTHORS
-- meaning "dependency bot" exactly as before. And
-- distinguishing an UNSET file from a deliberately empty one (every reader
-- pre-declares BOT_AUTHORS=() before sourcing, so the two are indistinguishable
-- today) would collapse "an empty list means three things" into one rule plus
-- a table of per-reader defaults. Both touch all three scripts.

-- == Release bots get their own tag ==
--
-- bots.conf carries a SECOND list, RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS, naming the bots that
-- open release PRs (release-please and its kin; at Kognic
-- `app/kognic-gha-release-bot`, PR author login
-- `kognic-gha-release-bot[bot]`, titles "chore(master): release X.Y.Z"). Its
-- entry form, and the check that enforces it, are BOT_AUTHORS' own (see "A
-- malformed BOT_AUTHORS entry is fatal, not silently misread" below). The
-- shipped value is empty; bots.conf documents `app/kognic-gha-release-bot` as
-- the example.
--
-- fetch-reviews.sh is the reader that uses it. Its bot-author pass queries the union of the
-- two lists, so a release bot's open PRs reach the board whether or not the
-- user is a requested reviewer on them, and to_feed_items tags a PR whose
-- author is on RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS `tag "release-please"`, matching the author
-- the same way BOT_AUTHORS is matched ("app/<slug>" against "<slug>[bot]",
-- case-insensitively). fetch-dependabot.sh reads it for one thing only, the
-- overlap check in "A bot on both lists is fatal" below; it still lists
-- dependency bots alone, and a release PR is not one.
--
-- THE TAG COMES FROM THE AUTHOR CONFIG, NEVER FROM THE "[bot]" SUFFIX. The
-- suffix says a bot wrote the PR, not which kind of bot; deriving a runbook
-- from it is the conflation "Which bot earns the dependency runbook" exists to
-- undo. So an unlisted release bot gets pr_review (or, with BOT_AUTHORS empty,
-- the suffix fallback's dependabot), exactly as before this list existed —
-- release_please is only ever earned by being named. And the release list is
-- checked FIRST: a listed release bot is never handed the dependency runbook
-- by the empty-BOT_AUTHORS suffix fallback, because naming it is the
-- deployment saying what it is. A bot listed on BOTH lists never reaches this
-- ordering: the conf is refused outright (see "A bot on both lists is fatal"
-- below).
--
-- ROUTING IS UNCHANGED. A release bot's login ends in "[bot]", so its PR
-- carries the author_bot signal and routes to the Bots sub-epic (feeds.allium:
-- RouteSignals rule 2), unless the user has engaged with it, in which case it
-- routes to My Reviews as any engaged bot PR does (rule 1). The new list
-- changes the TAG, and through it the runbook (dispatch-prompt.allium:
-- RenderReleasePrRunbook); it adds no Signal and no routing rule.
--
-- release_please is a review tag (core.allium: "THE REVIEW SET"), so a
-- release item falls under AReviewTaggedFeedItemNamesItsPr (feeds.allium):
-- its url must type as pr, which the bot-author pass satisfies by
-- construction, since its url comes straight from `gh`.
--
-- AN EXISTING bots.conf IS NOT REWRITTEN. The conf is installed
-- create-new-only (feeds.allium: InstallShippedFeedConfigs), so a deployment
-- that already has one never sees the new list documented. Both readers
-- pre-declare RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS=() before sourcing the file, as they do
-- BOT_AUTHORS, so an old conf reads as an empty list and each script behaves
-- exactly as it did before; a deployment opts in by adding the array itself.


-- == A malformed BOT_AUTHORS entry is fatal, not silently misread ==
--
-- bots.conf documents exactly ONE entry form, gh search's app form:
-- "app/<slug>", matching ^app/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$. Both readers now VALIDATE
-- every entry against that form immediately after sourcing the file, and
-- both EXIT NON-ZERO on the first cycle in which any entry fails.
--
-- WHAT WENT WRONG WITHOUT IT. The two readers consume the same entry in two
-- incompatible ways, and both failed silently on an entry written the other
-- natural way, "dependabot[bot]":
--
--   - fetch-reviews.sh derives its dependency-bot author logins by stripping
--     a leading "app/" and appending "[bot]" unconditionally, so the entry
--     became "dependabot[bot][bot]". It matched no PR author, and every one
--     of that bot's PRs was tagged pr_review instead of dependabot — handed
--     the wrong runbook, with no warning anywhere.
--   - the bot-author pass and fetch-dependabot.sh pass the entry straight to
--     gh, which wants the app form too, so the same entry also made those
--     queries return nothing.
--
-- Both symptoms are indistinguishable from "this bot has no open PRs", which
-- is the one reading a user cannot disprove by looking at the board.
--
-- WHY FATAL RATHER THAN A WARNING. A malformed entry is a typo in a
-- hand-edited config file: it is not transient, it will not fix itself, and
-- every cycle it survives produces a board that looks complete and is not.
-- The two softer shapes were rejected. Warning and keeping the entry leaves
-- the wrong board in place and relies on someone reading stderr. Warning and
-- DROPPING the entry is worse still: an all-invalid list then shrinks to
-- empty, and an empty list already means three different fallbacks (see
-- "Which bot earns the dependency runbook" above) — a typo would quietly arm
-- fetch-dependabot.sh's app/kognic-renovate default and fetch-reviews.sh's
-- "[bot]"-suffix tagging, which is the original silent misreading wearing a
-- different hat.
--
-- The cost is accepted and named: the cycle emits NOTHING, so unrelated
-- review cards disappear from the board until the conf is fixed. That is the
-- point. An empty board is a question the user asks; a subtly wrong one is
-- not. On the auto-poll path this lands on feeds.allium's FeedCommandFailure
-- bucket 2 (non-zero exit), which logs at warn and bumps last_run; the
-- diagnostic surface is `dispatch verify-feed`, which prints the script's
-- stderr and exits 1.
--
-- EVERY BAD ENTRY IS REPORTED, not just the first. The validation walks the
-- whole list, prints one named line per offender to stderr, and only then
-- exits. A conf with three typos costs one fix round-trip, not three.
--
-- RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS IS VALIDATED EXACTLY THE SAME WAY, by the reader that
-- uses its entries, fetch-reviews.sh (fetch-dependabot.sh only compares them
-- against BOT_AUTHORS, below, and does not check their form): the same app form, the same fatal non-zero exit, every
-- offender named on its own stderr line, and the line names the list the
-- entry sits in. Both lists are walked before the script exits, so a conf
-- with a typo in each still costs one round-trip. A malformed release entry
-- fails silently the same two ways a malformed BOT_AUTHORS one did: the
-- bot-author pass queries nothing for it, and no PR author matches it, so the
-- release bot's PRs drop back to pr_review (or vanish when no review query
-- matched them) with nothing saying why.
--
-- THE VALIDATION LIVES IN THE SCRIPTS, NOT IN bots.conf. The conf is
-- installed create-new-only (see feeds.allium's InstallShippedFeedConfigs),
-- so an existing deployment's copy is never rewritten by setup — and unlike
-- the SCRIPTS, which feeds.allium's InstallShippedFeedScripts does keep up to
-- date, there is no branch at all that rewrites a conf. A check shipped
-- inside the conf would reach only deployments that never had one — which is
-- exactly the set that has nothing to validate. Each reader therefore carries
-- its own copy of the check; there is no shared library, because these are
-- standalone user-owned executables installed independently.
--
-- THE SHIPPED STATE NEVER FAILS. An absent bots.conf leaves empty lists,
-- and an empty list has nothing to validate and overlaps nothing: both
-- readers behave exactly as before. fetch-dependabot.sh's empty-list fallback, app/kognic-renovate, is
-- itself in the legal form, so the fallback path cannot trip the check.
--
-- A BOT RUNNING UNDER A PLAIN USER ACCOUNT IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE, deliberately.
-- A bare "<login>" entry — a bot with no "[bot]" suffix — would need to be
-- passed to gh verbatim AND matched as a PR author verbatim, giving the file
-- a second entry form that both readers must agree on. That is the same
-- hazard the strict single contract exists to avoid: one entry, two meanings,
-- depending on which reader sees it. Nothing supports such an account today
-- and nothing is being added to; a deployment that needs one wants a spec
-- decision of its own, not a widened regex.

-- == A bot on both lists is fatal ==
--
-- An entry that appears in BOTH BOT_AUTHORS and RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS is a
-- FATAL configuration error, in both readers of bots.conf: fetch-reviews.sh
-- and fetch-dependabot.sh. Each compares the two lists immediately after
-- sourcing the file, alongside the malformed-entry check above, and on any
-- overlap names every overlapping entry on its own stderr line, exits
-- non-zero and emits NOTHING — exactly as a malformed entry does, and with
-- the same accepted cost: the cycle's cards disappear until the conf is
-- fixed. Entries are compared the way an author is matched,
-- case-insensitively, so "app/Foo" and "app/foo" overlap.
--
-- WHY FATAL. The two lists claim opposite things about one bot — "it opens
-- dependency bumps" and "it opens releases" — and the readers would resolve
-- the contradiction differently, silently. fetch-reviews.sh checks the
-- release list first and tags the bot's PRs release-please; fetch-dependabot.sh
-- lists the same PRs as dependency bumps and tags them dependabot. The one PR
-- would reach two different runbooks depending on which script polled it,
-- and either answer may be the wrong one. Only the deployment knows which
-- list it meant, so the scripts refuse to guess, for the reasons "WHY FATAL
-- RATHER THAN A WARNING" above gives for a typo.
--
-- fetch-dependabot.sh reads RELEASE_BOT_AUTHORS for this check alone. It does
-- not validate that list's form — a malformed release entry is
-- fetch-reviews.sh's to report — and it never lists PRs by it.
--
-- THE LISTS ARE COMPARED AS CONFIGURED, before any fallback. An empty
-- BOT_AUTHORS overlaps nothing: fetch-dependabot.sh's app/kognic-renovate
-- default and fetch-reviews.sh's "[bot]"-suffix tagging are defaults, not
-- listings, so they never trip the check.
